import{c as e,Q as a,j as o,m as r}from"./chunks/framework.BXc4yaFE.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Workflows are King","description":"","frontmatter":{"url":"https://x.com/jaminball/status/2067981638227964220","title":"Workflows are King","author":"Jamin Ball","archived":"2026-06-19T00:00:00.000Z","handle":"@jaminball","published":"2026-06-19T00:00:00.000Z","stats":"108 likes · 9 retweets · 268 bookmarks · 17926 views","tags":["ai-strategy","workflows","moat","saas","agentic-ai","orchestration","startup-strategy"],"type":"twitter-article"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-19-@jaminball-964220.md","filePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-19-@jaminball-964220.md","lastUpdated":1785909234000}'),s={name:"research/tweets/2026-06-19-@jaminball-964220.md"};function n(i,t,l,h,d,w){return a(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[r('<h1 id="workflows-are-king-—-jaminball" tabindex="-1">Workflows are King — @jaminball <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows-are-king-—-jaminball" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workflows are King — @jaminball&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>來源</strong>：<a href="https://x.com/jaminball/status/2067981638227964220" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://x.com/jaminball/status/2067981638227964220</a><strong>作者</strong>：Jamin Ball（@jaminball） <strong>發布日期</strong>：2026-06-19 <strong>收錄日期</strong>：2026-06-19 <strong>類型</strong>：Twitter Article <strong>統計</strong>：108 likes · 9 retweets · 268 bookmarks · 17926 views</p><hr><h2 id="全文" tabindex="-1">全文 <a class="header-anchor" href="#全文" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;全文&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From Clouded Judgement: Workflows are King</p><p>Workflows workflows workflows…I&#39;ve probably written about this in the past (hard to keep track of everything I&#39;ve written at this point, so I&#39;m sure there will be some repetitive content!) but I wanted to circle back to it.</p><p>For SaaS companies, the conventional wisdom was that you developed a moat if you built a system of record - a platform that stored data. If you owned / controlled the data, you had a moat! Data has gravity. True, but that wasn&#39;t the real moat. The real moat was the hundreds of workflows that grabbed data from that system of record and then got work done. Sometimes those workflows originated from the system of record platform itself. Sometimes the workflows originated elsewhere, and one stop of the workflow grabbed data from the system of record.</p><p>The real moat is owning the platform that the most workflows touched (and the platforms the most workflows touched was almost always the system of record). Swapping out the system of record was nearly impossible because it didn&#39;t just mean porting the data to a new system of record, it meant rebuilding / verifying / testing / securing / etc ALL of the workflows. Many of these workflows were in the critical path or customer facing. The cost of going through this change management was nearly always greater than the value of swapping to a new system of record.</p><p>I think the same thing is repeating with AI, with a twist. In SaaS, the moat lived at the data layer. Workflows formed around to the system of record, and this is what made it so hard to rip out. With agents, we still have workflows, but those workflows start to become more dynamic vs static (for SaaS workflows). So now the moat / anchor moves up a layer. The moat isn&#39;t a derivative of where the data sits (ie workflows touching data), it&#39;s where the work gets orchestrated. This is similar to what I wrote about last week about the value of becoming the Clearinghouse.</p><p>So what would my take away be for founders? I think the instinct when you read this (the moat is now the orchestration) is to go try to build the orchestration layer from the start. I don&#39;t think this should be the takeaway! At least that&#39;s not how systems of record because what they did. Salesforce wasn&#39;t the platform a thousand workflows touched from day 1. They started with something more narrow / niche, owned a single use case, got really good at that one thing, and then slowly expanded outward until more and more workflows were built around them.</p><p>I believe the agentic version will have the same playbook (albeit from a different starting point). Pick a single workflow (probably something that looks like it&#39;s about to get commoditized), something that will become more important in the future but appears niche today (you want to be riding the right wave), and do this much better than anyone else. People will assume there&#39;s no defensibility in this one niche (and why you&#39;ll get underestimated).</p><p>Then use this one workflow / wedge as strategic real estate. Build other workflows around it that are adjacent. Then slowly build the orchestration layer around it all (the thing that manages, routes, and governs the agents doing the work). One of my favorite sayings is &quot;startups aren&#39;t static.&quot; What you do today isn&#39;t the only thing you&#39;ll do. You&#39;ll expand, go deeper in certain areas, etc. What&#39;s important is that where you start is strategic real estate you can build around. Then you earn the right to &quot;manage&quot; (ie orchestrate) all of these workflows (ie agents)</p><p>The end state really is a new kind of system of record. In SaaS, the database held data and the moat formed around it in the form of workflows. In an agentic world, the &quot;database&quot; from the SaaS world becomes workflows. And the company that owns where those workflows get orchestrated I think will own the next moat.</p><hr><h2 id="研究摘要" tabindex="-1">研究摘要 <a class="header-anchor" href="#研究摘要" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;研究摘要&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>TL;DR</strong>：AI 時代護城河上移到工作流「編排層」——從 SaaS 系統記錄的類比推演出 agentic startup 的進入策略（single workflow wedge → expand → earn orchestration）。</p><h3 id="核心論點" tabindex="-1">核心論點 <a class="header-anchor" href="#核心論點" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;核心論點&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>SaaS 真正護城河 = 工作流，非數據</strong>：替換系統記錄需重建所有工作流，切換成本 &gt;&gt; 替換價值</li><li><strong>AI 時代護城河上移一層</strong>：從「工作流圍繞數據」→「工作流被誰編排」；moat 在 orchestration layer</li><li><strong>Founders 不應急建 orchestration</strong>：Salesforce 也非 day 1 就是工作流樞紐——從單一 niche workflow 切入</li><li><strong>策略房地產原則</strong>：選看似要被商品化但實際將更重要的 workflow 作為起點，向外擴張相鄰 workflow</li><li><strong>終態</strong>：agentic 世界的「系統記錄」= 工作流本身；擁有 orchestration 的公司擁有下一個護城河</li></ul><h3 id="關鍵數據-工具" tabindex="-1">關鍵數據 / 工具 <a class="header-anchor" href="#關鍵數據-工具" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;關鍵數據 / 工具&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Salesforce 歷史案例：單一 CRM 用例起點 → 逐步成為千個工作流的樞紐</li><li>&quot;Clearinghouse&quot; 概念：作者上週文章的延伸，成為結算中心即成為 orchestration 所在地</li><li>VC 視角（Clouded Judgement newsletter，Jamin Ball = 知名 B2B SaaS VC 分析師）</li></ul><h3 id="批判性觀察" tabindex="-1">批判性觀察 <a class="header-anchor" href="#批判性觀察" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;批判性觀察&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>類比成立但非原創</strong>：SaaS workflow moat → AI orchestration moat 在 AI 敘事中已相當普遍，無新資料支撐</li><li><strong>操作指引偏薄</strong>：「選看似商品化但重要的 workflow」原則正確，但如何判斷無具體框架（哪些特徵代表「戰略地產」？）</li><li><strong>護城河轉移風險未討論</strong>：若 foundation model capabilities 跳躍，現有 workflow layer 可能被原生 AI 工具整碗端走，非緩慢轉移</li></ul><hr><h2 id="評分" tabindex="-1">評分 <a class="header-anchor" href="#評分" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;評分&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>維度</th><th>分數</th><th>說明</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A. Workspace 可行動性 (30%)</td><td>5/10</td><td>VC 策略視角，無直接可用 prompt / 工具配置，需轉譯才可行動</td></tr><tr><td>B. 創新性 (20%)</td><td>6/10</td><td>SaaS→AI 護城河類比有清晰度，但核心論點非全新</td></tr><tr><td>C. 證據品質 (20%)</td><td>5/10</td><td>全為類比推理，無量化數據；Salesforce 案例為常識非新證據</td></tr><tr><td>D. 技術深度 (15%)</td><td>4/10</td><td>VC newsletter 文體，商業策略層，無技術實現細節</td></tr><tr><td>E. 泛化性 (15%)</td><td>8/10</td><td>框架適用任何 B2B SaaS/AI startup 策略思考</td></tr><tr><td><strong>加權總分</strong></td><td><strong>5.5/10</strong></td><td>5×0.3+6×0.2+5×0.2+4×0.15+8×0.15</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>整合決策</strong>：Skip（僅歸檔） <strong>理由</strong>：總分 5.5 未達 6 分門檻；策略視角有參考價值但對 workspace 無直接可行動輸出。</p>',26)])])}const c=e(s,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,c as default};
